{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01CZtPcos7kWaPDW2yCW9oCo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Anchor is a **single-user personal productivity system** for J.C. Liechty that has grown a substantial **R&D project cockpit** on top of it. Grounded claims:\n\n**Core purpose â€” task/project management**\n- It's a GTD-style productivity tool: tasks and projects live as **plain markdown files** (`DASHBOARD.md`, `PROJECTS.md`, `INBOX.md`, domain files under `domains/`) â€” markdown is the single source of truth, no database.\n- It ships a **local web app** (`anchor_gui.py`) â€” a stdlib-only Python HTTP server bound to `http://localhost:8777` that generates the full dashboard HTML on each request and writes changes back to the markdown files via `/api/*` endpoints.\n- It runs **persistently as a Windows service** (NSSM service `anchor`, auto-start) on the host `PUTER`/`gwl-server`; you open the browser, you don't launch it by hand.\n- A **CLI engine** (`anchor.py`) mirrors the same logic (done/cancel/save/restore/add/capture/dashboard) and regenerates a static `dashboard.html`.\n- Tasks move through a lifecycle: Active â†’ Done â†’ Cancelled â†’ Saved-for-Later â†’ Restored, each archive reviewable.\n- A **daily 5 AM health check** (`anchor_healthcheck.py`, Task Scheduler) self-tests file integrity, every endpoint, and the R&D surfaces (all model calls stubbed), writing dated reports to `health_reports/`.\n\n**The larger half â€” an R&D \"Project Cockpit\"**\n- Layered on top is a control surface (now at **v4 \"Project Cockpit,\" built and merged to master 2026-06-13**) that turns each project into a mission-control window for the **trio workflow**: researchPrime (Research) â†’ Crucible (Planning) â†’ Foreman (Build).\n- Each project window shows **lane tiles with one honest status light** (green=working, amber=needs-you, red=crashed, grey=idle/done); clicking a tile **expands an inline panel in place** with a summary on top and a **real, resizable terminal below**.\n- It runs **real interactive ConPTY terminal sessions** server-side (`terminal_session.py` â†’ `pty_manager.py`, xterm.js over a hand-rolled WebSocket / SSE), each **isolated in its own git worktree**, surviving disconnect, with an **engine toggle** (Claude â†” Gemini, Gemini research-only).\n- Opening a session **feels like a normal Claude Code / Gemini session**: the lane's skill auto-loads **once**, greets **once**, then behaves normally (this fixed the v2 repeating-prompt bug).\n- It also ships: **cached, validated, grounded summaries** of sessions and projects; a **Grass Catchers** idea lane with promote-to-lane; **deliverable pin/declare and launch-by-type** (skill/tool/service/program/doc); **cost/tokens/time rollups**; stage **handoffs** between plan and build; and **runnable deliverable previews** on ephemeral ports hard-guarded â‰  8777.\n\n**Constraints it holds to**\n- **Stdlib-only**, with exactly **one declared native exception**: `pywinpty` (lazy import, `pty_manager.py` only, optional `[terminal]` extra) for the ConPTY terminals; `distro.py` enforces no other third-party imports.\n- Everything stays in **one folder** (`C:\\dev\\Anchor`), all files human-readable, version-controlled via local Git â€” no OneDrive, no syncing, no binary/compiled assets.\n- Branded as a **Ghost World Labs** tool \"powered by NextGen Nuclear â˜¢.\"\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: these claims are drawn from `CLAUDE.md` and the project docs (which describe intent and recorded build status), not from re-reading the source this turn â€” if you want, I can verify any specific claim against the actual code."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4412,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25213,"cache_read_input_tokens":20597,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25213},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_availab